course: dictionary
lesson: 6527
title: Feelings in words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A noun can name a feeling. Nervousness is the noun for the worried, uneasy feeling you get before something that is going to happen. Its root is nervous, and the ending -ness turns the feeling word into a naming word.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «Nervousness can make the voice shake and the hands fidget or tremble.» The shaking voice and fidgeting hands show what nervousness looks like from the outside, so the word names a feeling, not a thing you can touch.
explain: Test your meaning in the sentence. «Dogs wag their tails for many feelings, including nervousness and warning.» If you put worry or uneasiness in place of nervousness, the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: Nervousness means a feeling of being worried, uneasy or a bit scared about something that is going to happen.
fact: Nervousness often comes before tests, performances, a first day at a new school or a big match.
fact: When you feel nervous, your heart may beat fast, you may have «butterflies in your stomach», or your hands may shake.
fact: Nervousness is normal, and slow breathing, practice and talking to someone can help.
fact: Nervousness is an uncountable noun, so you say «a feeling of nervousness», not «nervousnesses».
exercise: Before her first piano recital, Mia felt a flutter of nervousness. What is nervousness? | *A feeling of being worried or uneasy about something that is going to happen | A tune played very slowly | A loud noise from the stage | A feeling of being very bored
exercise: Which sentence shows nervousness? | Sam fell asleep on the bus | Sam laughed and ate his lunch in the sun | *Sam's hands trembled as he waited to be called onto the stage | Sam ran happily across the field
exercise: Find and copy the noun that names the feeling in this sentence: «Nervousness before a big match is normal.» | big | normal | match | *nervousness
test: «Nervousness can make the voice shake.» What does nervousness mean here? | *Worry or unease | Being far away | A type of musical instrument | Being very hungry
test: The polygraph measures nervousness, but honest people get nervous too. What does this tell you? | Honest people never feel worried | *A lie-detector cannot always show who is lying | Polygraphs are always right | Nervousness always means someone is lying
exam: Which phrase uses the word nervousness correctly? | nervousnessing the test | *a feeling of nervousness before the test | two nervousnesses before the test | the nervousnesses of the test
